   2: """
   3: #372: the general-path word STORE ignored cpu->byte_order.
   4: #378: the LDM/STM fast path ignored it too -- polarity INVERTED.
   5: 
   6: The general path's word-store arm was UNCONDITIONALLY EMPTY -- its guard was
   7: `!A__B && !A__H && HOST_LITTLE_ENDIAN` and its only body was `#ifdef A__STRD`,
   8: but A__STRD implies A__H, which the guard excludes. So a plain word STR on an LE
   9: host emitted no bytes and memory_rw copied the register's HOST bytes to guest
  10: memory, never consulting byte order. The FAST path's word arm has always been
  11: order-aware, so one STR wrote guest order to a WARM page and host order to a
  12: COLD one -- the #342/#355 self-contradiction class -- and every `strt`, which is
  13: general-path on its first access per page (#366), was reversed.
  14: 
  15: Measured before the fix on -E barearm: a cold store of 0x11223344 read back
  16: 0x44332211. After #372's fix: 0x11223344.
  17: 
  18: #378 is the SIBLING WITH THE POLARITY INVERTED: the LDM/STM fast path emitted by
  19: generate_arm_multi.c moved raw host words (no byte-order term anywhere in the
  20: generator), while the bdt_load/bdt_store fallback (arm_pop/arm_push) swaps on
  21: BOTH its warm and cold arms. So for multi-transfers the WARM rows discriminate
  22: and the COLD rows are the already-correct controls -- exactly opposite to
  23: #372's rows above. The fix gates the fast path's INSTALLATION on
  24: cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN (sound because byte_order cannot change
  25: after translation: the CP15 endian-switch route exits the emulator, SETEND is
  26: undecoded, and every other write is machine-setup-time), so post-fix a BE guest
  27: takes bdt_* everywhere and warm == cold == architectural.
  28: 
  29: Measured before the fix on -E barearm: stmia of r1=0x11223344,r2=0x55667788 to
  30: a WARM page laid down 44 33 22 11 88 77 66 55; DDI 0100I Table A2-2 (p. A2-32,
  31: with A2.7.2 p. A2-33 making LDM/STM a series of ordinary word accesses)
  32: requires 11 22 33 44 55 66 77 88. After the fix: the architectural bytes.
  33: 
  34: THIS PROBE RUNS BOTH BYTE ORDERS.
  35:   * The BE rig is `-E barearm`, whose MACHINE_SETUP sets EMUL_BIG_ENDIAN
  36:     outright (machine_test.c) -- no config file, no ELF, no stub-order wrinkle.
  37:   * The LE rig is plain `-E testarm`. On LE the broken code was ACCIDENTALLY
  38:     RIGHT (host order == guest order), so LE rows discriminate NOTHING: they
  39:     are INVARIANCE CONTROLS, present to catch a fix that repairs BE by
  40:     breaking LE.
  41: 
  42: Every row carries an EXPLICIT kind (DISC/CTRL) -- #372's rows derived kind from
  43: "cold in name", which #378's inverted polarity would have turned into a lie.
  44: 
  45: The ldrb witnesses are the purest form, because a byte load has no byte order
  46: (data[0] in both branches), so they expose the raw memory layout with no
  47: dependence on any word path; each `buggy` column is the exact reverse of its
  48: `arch` column, so the mirror-image BE and LE groups cannot both be satisfied
  49: by a swapped expectation table. r10/r11 are printed as `sl`/`fp` -- the
  50: debugger's ARM_REG_NAMES have no r10/r11 spellings.
  51: 
  52: In the STORE-test programs r1 = 0x11223344 and r2 = 0x55667788 are built with
  53: immediates, never loaded (a load would add its own path under test); the LDM
  54: programs of course DO load them -- that is what they test -- so those rows
  55: zero both registers first as sentinels.
  56: WARM pages are seeded with an explicit-width `put w` (which maps AND warms:
  57: MMU off => ok-1 == 1 => host_store too; the polarity is proven from source --
  58: memory_rw gates update_translation_table on !no_exceptions, and `put b` passes
