1698: print("ARM_FLAGS_RESULT=%d/%d" % (sum(1 for r in rows if r), len(rows)))
GXEMUL-SEC\regress\arm_writeback_probe.py
   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: """Round 104 gate candidate: the ARM load/store base writeback (#19).
   3: 
   4: The template `cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c` computes one address, MASKS it for
   5: alignment, and then writes the base register back from the masked value. ARM ARM
   6: DDI 0100I computes the writeback from the UNMASKED Rn:
   7: 
   8:   A5.2.8 post-indexed:  address = Rn ;  Rn = Rn + offset_12
   9:   A5.2.5 pre-indexed:   address = Rn + offset_12 ;  Rn = address
  10:   A2-43: the truncation lives inside Memory[<address>,<size>], i.e. on the
  11:          address sent to memory -- never on the register.
  12: 
  13: There are EXACTLY FOUR writeback sites in the single-register path, established
  14: by enumeration: two `reg(ic->arg[0]) =` statements in `A__NAME__general` (the
  15: pre-index and post-index forms) and the matching pair in `A__NAME`'s fast path.
  16: #364 removed the numeric cites that used to stand here -- they had gone stale a
  17: THIRD time, in the very file whose #357 note below says a numeric cite here has
  18: gone stale twice. Grep for the statement, not a line.
  19: `A__NAME_PC` and every conditional variant (`__eq`, `__ne`,
  20: ...) merely CALL `A__NAME`; two review seats suspected the PC special case was a
  21: third copy and it is not. A review seat enumerated the instantiations from
  22: generate_arm_loadstore.c -- 8 p/u/w files x (8 mode-2 + 12 mode-3) = 160 handler
  23: families -- all funnelling through those four statements.
  24: 
  25: WHICH SITE EACH ROW REACHES  --  #364: THE TABLE BELOW USED TO BE INVERTED
  26: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  27: It claimed "the fast path only runs once the page is in the translation array,
  28: so a SINGLE-execution row measures the GENERAL path and nothing else", and
  29: attributed rows to general sites on that basis. MEASURED FALSE, and in the worst
  30: direction: NO row in this probe reached the general path AT ALL. A temporary
  31: marker at the top of `A__NAME__general` counted ZERO hits across all 17 rows --
  32: not only the `once()` rows but the `loop10` iterations and both `warmed` passes.
  33: 
  34: The cause is this probe's own seeding. `put w` routes through
  35: `store_32bit_word` -> `memory_rw` with CACHE_DATA, and insertion into the
  36: translation array is gated on `!no_exceptions`, so seeding a page with `put w`
  37: ALREADY warms it: `host_load` is non-NULL before the guest executes a single
  38: instruction. `put b` is the opposite -- CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS -- and does
  39: not insert. The old note had these two backwards.
  40: 
  41: Consequence, stated plainly because two shipped rounds leaned on the old table:
  42: `#357`'s general-path writeback sites and `#362`'s general-path rotation were
  43: BOTH unmeasured, and each was independently confirmed deletable with the whole
  44: of gate 14 still green at 261 checks. The historical pre-fix sweep of 5/14 is
  45: entirely attributable to the fast path.
  46: 
  47: TWO AXES, kept apart, because conflating them is how the first draft of this very
  48: correction went wrong. A round of review found that draft claimed the new cold
  49: rows covered "general post-index writeback". They do not, and the reason is worth
  50: stating because it is easy to miss twice: `LDR_OFF0` is P=1/W=0, and the writeback
  51: is emitted only under (P and W) or (not P) -- so an offset form has NO WRITEBACK
  52: STATEMENT AT ALL, and `wb_addr` is not even declared for it. So:
  53: 
  54: WHICH ROWS REACH THE FOUR WRITEBACK SITES (the `reg(ic->arg[0]) =` statements)
  55:   fast    post-index  -- A, A2, B, F, S, R, G, `post4 algn`, `algn load data`,
  56:                          `byte post1 unal`, `wrap from zero`      (11 rows)
  57:   fast    pre-index   -- C (`pre4 unal gen`) and C2 (`pre4 unal fast`, BOTH
  58:                          passes, not just the second)              (2 rows)
  59:   general post-index  -- `ldrt post4 unal` (this probe), and the fold-marker
  60:                          probe's `copyin cold` / `copyout cold` arms
  61:   general pre-index   -- `cold pre4 unal` (this probe), and the strlen probe's
  62:                          `ldrb r3,[r4,#1]!` walk on each new page
  63: 
  64: #365 CORRECTED A CLAIM #364 MADE HERE. This table used to say both general
  65: writeback sites were UNCOVERED and that `#357`'s fix "STILL has no row reaching
  66: it". Measured false, and the correction is sharper than the claim: both sites
  67: were already REACHED, just not by rows in this file -- deleting the two general
  68: writeback statements turns 8 gate checks red in the strlen and fold-marker
  69: sections while this probe stays 20/20. What was missing was DISCRIMINATION.
  70: Rebuild with the faithful pre-`#357` bug (general writeback re-masked, fast pair
  71: intact) and the whole gate passed at 264: `ldrb` has datalen 1, so
  72: `addr &= ~(datalen - 1)` is the identity, and the fold arms use aligned bases.
  73: Reaching a statement without discriminating its correction measures nothing about
  74: it. The two `#365` rows are the first anywhere in the battery that do discriminate
  75: it, verified against that exact mutant.
  76: 
  77: ROWS WITH NO WRITEBACK SITE AT ALL (P=1/W=0 offset forms)
  78:   `pre4 no wb` and the three `rot word unal` rows -- in the FAST function
  79:   the three `rot word cold` rows                 -- in the GENERAL function
  80: 
  81: WHICH ROWS REACH THE GENERAL FUNCTION AT ALL, which is a different question and
  82: the one this round actually answers: the three `rot word cold` rows, and nothing
  83: else. They exercise `A__NAME__general`'s load-and-rotate arm and the `memory_rw`
  84: slow path -- the first rows in this file's history to enter that function. That
  85: is a DATA site, not a writeback site, and the round's value is real either way:
  86: `#362`'s general-path rotation was deletable with the whole gate green before
  87: these rows existed.
  88: 
  89: Deliberately no line numbers here: the `#357` note below says a numeric cite in
  90: this file has gone stale twice, and the ones this table used to carry had gone
  91: stale a third time by the round that corrected it.
  92: 
  93: A general-path row therefore needs a page this probe never touches with `put w`.
  94: That is what COLD/COLD1/COLD2/COLD3 are for, and the three `cold` rotation rows
  95: are the first rows in this file's history to enter `A__NAME__general`.
  96: A guest STORE does NOT work for this and was tried: `update_translation_table`
  97: sets `host_load` unconditionally and gates only `host_store` on the write flag,
  98: so storing to a page warms it for loading too.
  99: 
 100: `put b` is not the ONLY cold seeding -- a review seat corrected an earlier draft
 101: that said so. The debugger's string modes `put s` and `put z` also write through
 102: `memory_rw` with `CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS` and leave a page equally cold. It
 103: is the flag that matters, not the command; `put b` is simply the convenient one
 104: for laying down four chosen bytes. (`put h`/`put w`/`put d` all reach CACHE_DATA
 105: via store_16/32/64bit_word, so all three warm.)
 106: 
 107: TWO INSERTION ROUTES THAT DO NOT OBEY THE `!no_exceptions` GATE, both found in
 108: review and both counter-intuitive enough to be worth naming here rather than
 109: leaving for the next person to trip over:
 110:   * THE DEVICE ARM INSERTS DESPITE NO_EXCEPTIONS. `memory_rw`'s device path gates
 111:     on `update_translation_table != NULL && !(ok & MEMORY_NOT_FULL_PAGE) &&
 112:     (devices[i].flags & DM_DYNTRANS_OK)` -- with NO `!no_exceptions` term. So a
 113:     `put b` to a fully-backed DM_DYNTRANS_OK device page WARMS it, and with
 114:     NO_EXCEPTIONS set the device handler is not even called, so the write is
 115:     dropped while the mapping is installed. Harmless at 0x20000 on testarm, whose
 116:     devices all sit at >= 0x10000000 -- but fatal to this mechanism if COLD ever
 117:     moves, and the assert below does NOT check for it.
 118:   * INSTRUCTION FETCH INSERTS WITH writeflag 0. quick_pc_to_pointers' generic
 119:     path calls update_translation_table when host_load is NULL, so EXECUTING on a
 120:     page warms it for LOADS (leaving host_store NULL). Irrelevant to COLD, which
 121:     is never executed, but it means "put b leaves a page cold" is a statement
 122:     about SEEDING and not about the page's whole life.
 123: Also contingent, and worth knowing before this is reused: with the MMU ON,
 124: arm_translate_v2p_mmu failure returns BEFORE the insertion gate, so `put w` would
 125: not warm an unmapped page at all. testarm never enables the MMU. And the
 126: translation array is a 384-entry round-robin TLB, so a row set touching more
 127: pages than that would silently re-cool a warmed page -- this probe's working set
 128: is about four pages, so the attribution here is a property of that, not a theorem.
 129: 
 130: Loads and stores are SEPARATE instantiations of this template, so a row set made
 131: only of loads cannot see a store-side regression at all: row S is a store
 132: livelock. Register-offset forms (`A__REG`) are a third family: row R.
 133: 
 134: THE HEADLINE IS A LOOP THAT CANNOT MAKE PROGRESS
 135: ------------------------------------------------
 136: When the post-index offset is not a multiple of the access size the masked
 137: writeback is a FIXED POINT. Word base 0x10001 with offset 1 masks to 0x10000,
 138: adds 1, and writes back 0x10001 -- the value it started from. No amount of guest
 139: patience recovers from that, which is why it is the headline rather than the
 140: one-off wrong values. Progress is asserted as an exact architectural value after
 141: an architecturally bounded iteration count, never as "how far did it get in T
 142: seconds": the loop is counted in r3, which is independent of r0.
 143: 
 144: WHAT IS MANDATED VERSUS MERELY PERMITTED
 145: ----------------------------------------
 146: A review seat supplied a precision worth keeping: the writeback is ARCHITECTURALLY
 147: MANDATED for word accesses, whose unaligned behaviour is defined. Unaligned
 148: halfword loads (A4.1.28) and doubleword accesses (A2.8) are UNPREDICTABLE prior
 149: to ARMv6, so there an unmasked writeback is permitted-and-consistent rather than
 150: required. Every row below except G is a word form, i.e. in mandated space. Row G
 151: is labelled DISC-M ("model") for exactly that reason: it pins the pseudocode
 152: model of A5.3.6, not a silicon mandate, and it asserts only the BASE -- an
 153: unaligned halfword load's DATA is UNPREDICTABLE and must never be asserted.
 154: 
 155: NO *UNALIGNED/WRITEBACK* LDRD/STRD ROW here, overruling three review seats:
 156: A2.8 makes a doubleword access UNPREDICTABLE prior to ARMv6 whenever the
 157: address is not doubleword-aligned, so every base that would exercise the `~7`
 158: mask makes the instruction unspecified. The general-path fix covers LDRD/STRD
 159: (the fast path chickens out to it unconditionally -- the
 160: `#if defined(A__LDRD) || defined(A__STRD)` at the top of `A__NAME` in
 161: cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c -- and the general path masks with `datalen - 1` == 7;
 162: NO LINE NUMBERS ON PURPOSE, see below) but that coverage cannot honestly
 163: be gated. #355 already taught this project not to assert on encodings the
 164: architecture declines to define. [#389: ALIGNED LDRD/STRD byte-order rows now
 165: exist in arm_endian_probe.py -- this note is scoped to the unaligned/writeback
 166: axis only; do not read it as a blanket denial (this section once carried
 167: exactly that mistake, see below).
 168: #389 pass-2: the line number that used to sit in the sentence above is GONE, not
 169: corrected. Its history is the argument: it was `:226-228` (stale), #389 "fixed"
 170: it to `:338-340` -- which was the PRE-#389 line, computed against the revision
 171: being corrected, and which now lands in the middle of the BE STRD store arm that
 172: same round ADDED -- and the second fix to `:374-376` would have drifted on the
 173: next insertion too. An Opus diff-review seat caught both. The template's own #357
 174: comment already says "deliberately no line numbers" for exactly this reason.
 175: Name the construct; the compiler will find it, a stale number will not.]
 176: 
 177: UNALIGNED-LOAD-DATA ROWS -- #364: THIS SECTION USED TO SAY THERE WERE NONE, and
 178: it was written when that was true. It argued that a PIN on the unrotated value
 179: would be "inverted by the round that fixes the rotation", i.e. rewritten by the
 180: change it exists to guard, so only an ALIGNED-base data row was safe.
 181: 
 182: That reasoning was sound and its conclusion is now obsolete: `#362` FIXED the
 183: rotation and added three DISC rows on the unrotated-vs-rotated pair (a DISC row
 184: states both values, so it is not inverted by the fix -- it is satisfied by it),
 185: and `#364` added three more from a cold page. Six rows now pin unaligned loaded
 186: data. The aligned-base row stays, and stays correct for its own reason: no
 187: rotation applies at offset 0 in any architecture version.
 188: 
 189: Kept rather than deleted because the ARCHITECTURE note is still the reference:
 190: ARMv5 and below with CP15 A == 0 rotate the aligned word right by 8 * addr[1:0]
 191: (A2.8 p. A2-38, A4.1.23), so a base of 0x10001 over the word 0x11223344 owes
 192: 0x44112233 and NOT the masked 0x11223344.
 193: """
 194: import os
 195: import pty
 196: import re
 197: import select
 198: import sys
 199: import time
 200: 
 201: BIN = sys.argv[1]
 202: CODE = 0x8000
 203: SRC = 0x10000
 204: 
 205: #  Encodings. Bits after the 010 class are P U B W L, verified against the
 206: #  ldrt form 0xe4b0a004 (`ldrt sl,[r0],#4`) that netbsd_copyin matches; the
 207: #  register-offset forms use class 011. The halfword layout is
 208: #  cond 000 P U I W L Rn Rd immH 1SH1 immL, checked against `ldrh r1,[r0]` ==
 209: #  0xe1d010b0. EVERY word below was confirmed through the emulator's own
 210: #  `unassemble` before this probe was run -- this project has shipped a
 211: #  mis-encoded row before, one of them a committed gate row that measured the
 212: #  wrong register for months.
 213: MOV_R0_10000 = 0xE3A00801   # mov  r0,#0x10000
 214: MOV_R0_0     = 0xE3A00000   # mov  r0,#0
 215: ADD_R0_1     = 0xE2800001   # add  r0,r0,#1
 216: MOV_R2_1     = 0xE3A02001   # mov  r2,#1
 217: MOV_R3_0     = 0xE3A03000   # mov  r3,#0
 218: MOV_R3_1     = 0xE3A03001   # mov  r3,#1
 219: LDR_P1       = 0xE4901001   # ldr  r1,[r0],#1     post-index, +1
 220: LDR_P4       = 0xE4901004   # ldr  r1,[r0],#4     post-index, +4
 221: LDR_PRE4W    = 0xE5B01004   # ldr  r1,[r0,#4]!    pre-index + writeback
 222: LDR_PRE4     = 0xE5901004   # ldr  r1,[r0,#4]     pre-index, NO writeback
 223: LDR_M4       = 0xE4101004   # ldr  r1,[r0],#-4    post-index, -4  (U == 0)
 224: LDRB_P1      = 0xE4D01001   # ldrb r1,[r0],#1     byte: masks nothing
 225: LDRH_P1      = 0xE0D010B1   # ldrh r1,[r0],#1     halfword: masks ~1
 226: #  0xE480, not 0xE400: with L == 0 the U bit sits in the same nibble, so the
 227: #  obvious guess 0xe4002001 disassembles as `str r2,[r0],#-1` -- a NEGATIVE
 228: #  offset. Caught by `unassemble` before the sweep; it would have produced an
 229: #  UNEXPECTED value and sent the round debugging the emulator.
 230: STR_P1       = 0xE4802001   # str  r2,[r0],#1     STORE post-index, +1
 231: LDR_POST_R2  = 0xE6901002   # ldr  r1,[r0],r2     post-index, REGISTER offset
 232: ADD_R3_1     = 0xE2833001   # add  r3,r3,#1
 233: SUBS_R3_1    = 0xE2533001   # subs r3,r3,#1
 234: CMP_R3_10    = 0xE353000A   # cmp  r3,#10
 235: SPIN         = 0xEAFFFFFE   # b .
 236: 
 237: #  #365: the T form -- P=0/U=1/B=0/W=1/L=1, class 010. CHECKED through
 238: #  `unassemble` as `ldrt r1,[r0],#4`, and it lands in
 239: #  tmp_arm_loadstore_p0_u1_w1.c, handler arm_instr_load_w1_word_u1_p0_imm
 240: #  (generator index reg*512+p*256+u*128+b*64+w*32+l*16+c, so
 241: #  ((iword >> 16) & 0x3f0) + cond = 176 + 14 = 190).
 242: #
 243: #  WHY A T FORM RATHER THAN A COLD PAGE: this reaches A__NAME__general BY
 244: #  CONSTRUCTION and is immune to warming. The template tests
 245: #  is_userpage[addr >> 17] BEFORE the `page == NULL` test, and a clear bit calls
 246: #  A__NAME__general regardless of how warm the page is. The bit is set only when
 247: #  update_translation_table receives MEMORY_USER_ACCESS, which only this T-form
 248: #  family passes -- so a `put w` page is warm and the first `ldrt` still goes
 249: #  general. That cannot be silently voided by a future seeding change, which a
 250: #  cold page can be (see the device-arm and instruction-fetch notes above).
 251: #
 252: #  Rd is r1 DELIBERATELY, not r9: `0xE4B09004` is the word that arms
 253: #  COMBINE(netbsd_copyin), so an r9 destination would put this row in the
 254: #  instruction-combiner's path and measure something else entirely.
 255: LDRT_P4      = 0xE4B01004           # ldrt r1,[r0],#4   post-index +4, T bit
 256: 
 257: UNAL = [MOV_R0_10000, ADD_R0_1]     # base 0x10001
 258: ADD_R0_2     = 0xE2800002           # add  r0,r0,#2
 259: ADD_R0_3     = 0xE2800003           # add  r0,r0,#3
 260: LDR_OFF0     = 0xE5901000           # ldr  r1,[r0]   pre-index, offset 0, no wb
 261: UNAL2 = [MOV_R0_10000, ADD_R0_2]    # base 0x10002
 262: UNAL3 = [MOV_R0_10000, ADD_R0_3]    # base 0x10003
 263: ALIGN = [MOV_R0_10000]              # base 0x10000
 264: ZERO = [MOV_R0_0]                   # base 0
 265: 
 266: #  #364: a page this probe seeds with `put b` and NEVER with `put w`, so its
 267: #  translation entry stays absent and a load from it takes the GENERAL path.
 268: #  That is the whole point -- see the seeding note in run() and the corrected
 269: #  site table above. 0xE3A00802 is `mov r0,#0x20000`, CHECKED THROUGH
 270: #  `unassemble` rather than derived: same rot-8 immediate field as the verified
 271: #  MOV_R0_10000 (0xE3A00801) with imm8 stepped 1 -> 2.
 272: #
 273: #  THE SEEDING MECHANISM, cited from source rather than asserted. A review seat
 274: #  made the fair point that the previous note here stated this mechanism in
 275: #  several places without a citation -- and since the note it replaced was
 276: #  itself inverted, leaning on an uncited claim a second time would repeat the
 277: #  original mistake one level up. So, the four links:
 278: #    put b  -> debugger_cmds.c  memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE, CACHE_NONE|NO_EXCEPTIONS)
 279: #    put w  -> debugger_cmds.c  store_32bit_word()
 280: #           -> memory.c         cpu->memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE, CACHE_DATA)
 281: #    insertion gate, memory_rw.c:
 282: #           if (cpu->update_translation_table != NULL && !dyntrans_device_danger
 283: #               && !(ok & MEMORY_NOT_FULL_PAGE) && !no_exceptions)
 284: #                   cpu->update_translation_table(...)
 285: #  So NO_EXCEPTIONS blocks insertion outright, which is why `put b` leaves the
 286: #  page cold and `put w` does not. Note the gate ALSO requires a full page, so
 287: #  a device-backed or partial-page target would stay cold under `put w` too --
 288: #  irrelevant here (plain RAM), but it is the other way this can surprise.
 289: COLD = 0x20000
 290: MOV_R0_20000 = 0xE3A00802           # mov  r0,#0x20000
 291: COLD1 = [MOV_R0_20000, ADD_R0_1]    # base 0x20001, page never warmed
 292: COLD2 = [MOV_R0_20000, ADD_R0_2]    # base 0x20002
 293: COLD3 = [MOV_R0_20000, ADD_R0_3]    # base 0x20003
 294: 
 295: #  #364: assert nothing this probe warms shares COLD's page. A review seat found
 296: #  the hole: nothing enforced the inequality, so if a future edit moved CODE, SRC
 297: #  or the row-K address onto COLD's page, the program-write loop would warm it
 298: #  BEFORE `pc=` was sent -- and the three cold rows would still PASS, because the
 299: #  fast path yields the same rotated answer. Silent vacuity, in the exact shape
 300: #  this round exists to correct, merely pushed one step away.
 301: #
 302: #  Evaluated ONCE at import, not inside run(): a second review pass pointed out
 303: #  that inside run() it fires 20 times, each time AFTER forking an emulator and
 304: #  sending seeds, so a failure would leak a child and a pty master and would not
 305: #  fire until the first fork. Here it fires before any process exists.
 306: #
 307: #  Failure mode, stated so nobody misdiagnoses it: an AssertionError means no
 308: #  WRITEBACK_RESULT= line, which makes gate_arm.sh call gate_skip, and gate_skip
 309: #  exits EXIT_SKIP -- so every check AFTER the writeback section stops running and
 310: #  gate 14 reports SKIP, which reads like a missing rig image rather than a
 311: #  broken invariant. (That gate_skip swallows already-recorded failures is itself
 312: #  a separate defect, recorded in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.)
 313: #
 314: #  The 4 KB mask is not an assumption: ARM_ADDR_TO_PAGENR shifts by 12,
 315: #  tmp_arm_head.c sets DYNTRANS_PAGESIZE 4096, N_VPH32_ENTRIES is 2^32/2^12, and
 316: #  memory_rw inserts at `vaddr & ~offset_mask` with offset_mask 0xfff for ARM.
 317: #  What this guard does NOT cover, per the notes above: COLD landing on a
 318: #  DM_DYNTRANS_OK device page, which inserts even under NO_EXCEPTIONS.
 319: _PG = ~0xFFF
 320: for _n, _a in (("CODE", CODE), ("CODE end", CODE + 4 * 15),
 321:                ("SRC", SRC), ("SRC end", SRC + 4 * 7),
 322:                ("row K", 0x0), ("testarm stub", 64 * 1048576 - 4096 + 32)):
 323:     assert (_a & _PG) != (COLD & _PG), \
 324:         "%s 0x%x shares COLD's page 0x%x -- it would be warmed" % (_n, _a, COLD)
 325: 
 326: 
 327: def _br(cond, frm, to):
 328:     return cond | ((to - (frm + 2)) & 0xFFFFFF)
 329: 
 330: 
 331: def once(setup, body):
 332:     """One execution of `body`, then spin.
 333: 
 334:     #364: this used to claim "Reaches the GENERAL path only." MEASURED FALSE --
 335:     it reaches the general path NEVER. A marker at the top of A__NAME__general
 336:     counted zero hits on all 17 rows. The reason is this probe's own seeding:
 337:     `put w` goes through store_32bit_word -> memory_rw with CACHE_DATA, which
 338:     inserts the page into the translation array, so by the time the guest runs,
 339:     `host_load` is already non-NULL and even a single execution takes the FAST
 340:     path. Use COLD1/2/3 for a general-path row -- those pages are seeded with
 341:     `put b`, which uses CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS and does not insert.
 342:     """
 343:     return list(setup) + list(body) + [SPIN]
 344: 
 345: 
 346: def loop10(setup, body):
 347:     """`body` ten times, counted in r3 so the count cannot depend on r0.
 348: 
 349:     #364: this used to claim iteration 1 takes the general path and iterations
 350:     2+ the fast one, i.e. "one row, both writeback sites". MEASURED FALSE -- the
 351:     page is ALREADY in the translation array before the guest runs, because the
 352:     probe seeds it with `put w`. Every iteration takes the fast path, and the
 353:     marker count for these rows is zero like all the others. One row, ONE site.
 354:     """
 355:     prog = list(setup) + [MOV_R3_0]
 356:     top = len(prog)
 357:     prog += list(body) + [ADD_R3_1, CMP_R3_10]
 358:     br = len(prog)
 359:     prog += [_br(0x1A000000, br, top), SPIN]      # bne -> top
 360:     return prog
 361: 
 362: 
 363: def warmed(body):
 364:     """Two passes, base RE-SEEDED each pass, so pass 2 is still unaligned.
 365: 
 366:     Without the re-seed the pre-index row self-heals: pass 1 leaves an ALIGNED
 367:     base, and pass 2 then has nothing to discriminate.
 368: 
 369:     #364: this used to claim pass 2 is "the only execution in this whole set
 370:     that reaches the fast pre-index site". MEASURED FALSE in the other direction
 371:     from once()/loop10 -- BOTH passes reach it, because the probe's `put w`
 372:     seeding warms the page before pass 1. The re-seed is still load-bearing for
 373:     the reason above; only the site claim was wrong.
 374:     """
 375:     prog = [MOV_R3_1]
 376:     top = len(prog)
 377:     prog += UNAL + list(body) + [SUBS_R3_1]
 378:     br = len(prog)
 379:     prog += [_br(0x5A000000, br, top), SPIN]      # bpl -> top
 380:     return prog
 381: 
 382: 
 383: #  #364: the column is `%-32s` followed by TWO LITERAL SPACES in the format
 384: #  string, not `%-34s` as this note used to say -- a review seat caught the
 385: #  discrepancy. It matters that the two spaces are literal: they are emitted
 386: #  unconditionally, so even a name that filled the whole 32-column field would
 387: #  still satisfy a two-space gate pattern. The longest name here is 26 chars
 388: #  ("A wb regofs post1 unal x10"); a draft of this very note said 24, which is
 389: #  the length of the six `rot word` names -- corrected in review, and worth
 390: #  correcting rather than shrugging at in a note whose whole subject is being
 391: #  exact about this trap.
 392: #  Names stay well inside that column, so a gate check of
 393: #  the form "<name>  *DISC" always sees at least two spaces. A name as long as
 394: #  its column makes such a check UNSATISFIABLE -- shipped once before.
 395: ROWS = [
 396:     # name, kind, program, register, buggy, arch
 397:     ("A wb word post1 unal x10", "DISC",
 398:      loop10(UNAL, [LDR_P1]), "r0", 0x10001, 0x1000B),
 399:     ("A wb word post1 algn x10", "DISC",
 400:      loop10(ALIGN, [LDR_P1]), "r0", 0x10001, 0x1000A),
 401:     ("A wb word post4 unal", "DISC",
 402:      once(UNAL, [LDR_P4]), "r0", 0x10004, 0x10005),
 403:     ("A wb word pre4 unal gen", "DISC",
 404:      once(UNAL, [LDR_PRE4W]), "r0", 0x10004, 0x10005),
 405:     ("A wb word pre4 unal fast", "DISC",
 406:      warmed([LDR_PRE4W]), "r0", 0x10004, 0x10005),
 407:     ("A wb word postneg4 unal", "DISC",
 408:      once(UNAL, [LDR_M4]), "r0", 0x0000FFFC, 0x0000FFFD),
 409:     ("A wb store post1 unal x10", "DISC",
 410:      loop10(UNAL, [STR_P1]), "r0", 0x10001, 0x1000B),
 411:     ("A wb regofs post1 unal x10", "DISC",
 412:      loop10(UNAL + [MOV_R2_1], [LDR_POST_R2]), "r0", 0x10001, 0x1000B),
 413:     ("A wb halfword post1 unal", "DISC-M",
 414:      once(UNAL, [LDRH_P1]), "r0", 0x10001, 0x10002),
 415:     ("A wb word post4 algn", "PIN",
 416:      once(ALIGN, [LDR_P4]), "r0", 0x10004, 0x10004),
 417:     ("A wb algn load data", "PIN",
 418:      once(ALIGN, [LDR_P4]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x11223344),
 419:     ("A wb byte post1 unal", "PIN",
 420:      once(UNAL, [LDRB_P1]), "r0", 0x10002, 0x10002),
 421:     ("A wb word pre4 no wb", "PIN",
 422:      once(ALIGN, [LDR_PRE4]), "r0", 0x10000, 0x10000),
 423:     ("A wb wrap from zero", "PIN",
 424:      once(ZERO, [LDR_M4]), "r0", 0xFFFFFFFC, 0xFFFFFFFC),
 425: 
 426:     #  #362: an unaligned word LOAD returns the aligned word ROTATED RIGHT by
 427:     #  8 * addr[1:0] (DDI 0100I A2.8, A4.1.23). This template masked and never
 428:     #  rotated. The word at 0x10000 is 0x11223344, so the three offsets owe
 429:     #  0x44112233, 0x33441122 and 0x22334411, and the "buggy" column is the
 430:     #  unrotated word -- which is what the pre-fix build returned in all three.
 431:     #
 432:     #  These rows IDENTIFY the model rather than merely failing an expectation:
 433:     #  mask-only, rotate, and the ARMv6 U == 1 true-unaligned access are pairwise
 434:     #  distinct at every nonzero offset, so a wrong answer says WHICH model is
 435:     #  implemented. Measured MASK-only on all three pre-fix, ROTATE post-fix.
 436:     #
 437:     #  Offset 0 is deliberately absent: rotation by zero is the identity, so such
 438:     #  a row could never fail, and the aligned case is already pinned elsewhere.
 439:     ("A wb rot word unal plus1", "DISC",
 440:      once(UNAL, [LDR_OFF0]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x44112233),
 441:     ("A wb rot word unal plus2", "DISC",
 442:      once(UNAL2, [LDR_OFF0]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x33441122),
 443:     ("A wb rot word unal plus3", "DISC",
 444:      once(UNAL3, [LDR_OFF0]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x22334411),
 445: 
 446:     #  #364: the same three offsets, from a page seeded ONLY with `put b`, so the
 447:     #  translation entry is absent and the load takes the GENERAL path. These are
 448:     #  the first rows in this file to enter `A__NAME__general` -- see the
 449:     #  corrected site table at the top.
 450:     #
 451:     #  Why they are not redundant with the three rows above, which is the whole
 452:     #  finding: with `#362`'s general-path rotation neutralised and its fast-path
 453:     #  rotation left intact, the rows above stayed GREEN and the whole of gate 14
 454:     #  passed at 261 checks. These three go red, reading the unrotated
 455:     #  0x11223344. The two triples differ in exactly one thing -- the seeding
 456:     #  width of the page they read -- which is what pins the mechanism: on ONE
 457:     #  binary, `put b` gave ls_general=1 and the mask-only answer while `put w`
 458:     #  gave ls_general=0 and the rotated answer.
 459:     ("A wb rot word cold plus1", "DISC",
 460:      once(COLD1, [LDR_OFF0]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x44112233),
 461:     ("A wb rot word cold plus2", "DISC",
 462:      once(COLD2, [LDR_OFF0]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x33441122),
 463:     ("A wb rot word cold plus3", "DISC",
 464:      once(COLD3, [LDR_OFF0]), "r1", 0x11223344, 0x22334411),
 465: 
 466:     #  #365: the first two rows anywhere in the battery that DISCRIMINATE #357's
 467:     #  general-path correction. The distinction matters and cost a refuted
 468:     #  prediction to find: the two general writeback SITES were already being
 469:     #  REACHED before this round -- the strlen probe's `ldrb r3,[r4,#1]!` is
 470:     #  P=1/W=1 and takes the general pre-index arm on each new page, and the
 471:     #  fold-marker probe's `copyin cold` / `copyout cold` arms are six ldrt/strt
 472:     #  with the is_userpage bit left clear, and their r0/r1-advanced-by-24
 473:     #  witness IS a writeback assertion. Deleting both statements turns 8 gate
 474:     #  checks red in THOSE sections.
 475:     #
 476:     #  #366 corrected a detail this comment got wrong when it shipped: it said
 477:     #  ALL SIX of those ldrt/strt run in A__NAME__general. Only the FIRST does.
 478:     #  The general path's own memory_rw insert SETS the user bit --
 479:     #  cpu_dyntrans.c does `if (useraccess) is_userpage[index >> 5] |= 1 <<
 480:     #  (index & 31)`, and the found-entry arm clears-then-sets it -- so the
 481:     #  second ldrt on that page finds the bit set and takes the FAST path. The
 482:     #  fold-marker probe's own docstring already said as much ("only pass 2 and
 483:     #  later can fold"). The count is ONE general entry per cold arm, not six.
 484:     #  Nothing else here changes: the arms still reach the site, and the 8-red
 485:     #  measurement stands.
 486:     #
 487:     #  But nothing DISCRIMINATED the mask. Rebuild with the faithful pre-#357
 488:     #  bug -- the general writeback re-masked, `addr` instead of `wb_addr`, fast
 489:     #  pair intact -- and the whole of gate 14 passed at 264 checks, 0 failures.
 490:     #  Every row that reached those sites was blind to it: `ldrb` has
 491:     #  datalen 1, so `addr &= ~(datalen - 1)` is the IDENTITY, and the fold arms
 492:     #  use aligned bases 0x10000/0x11000 where masked and unmasked agree. So the
 493:     #  honest statement is "undiscriminated", not "unreached" -- and a row that
 494:     #  reaches a statement without discriminating its correction measures nothing
 495:     #  about it.
 496:     #
 497:     #  Measured on four builds. Pristine: both ok. General writeback DELETED:
 498:     #  both red at the un-incremented base. General writeback RE-MASKED (the real
 499:     #  #357 bug): both red at exactly the predicted masked values. POST-index
 500:     #  only: the ldrt row red and the cold pre4 row green, which separates the
 501:     #  two sites -- as it must, since P=0/W=1 and P=1/W=1 are distinct
 502:     #  translation units each compiling only its own #ifdef arm.
 503:     ("A wb word ldrt post4 unal", "DISC",
 504:      once(UNAL, [LDRT_P4]), "r0", 0x10004, 0x10005),
 505:     ("A wb word cold pre4 unal", "DISC",
 506:      once(COLD1, [LDR_PRE4W]), "r0", 0x20004, 0x20005),
 507: ]
 508: 
 509: 
 510: def run(prog, regs):
 511:     stub = "/tmp/r104c_stub.bin"
 512:     with open(stub, "wb") as f:
 513:         f.write((0xE1A00000).to_bytes(4, "little"))
 514:     pid, fd = pty.fork()
 515:     if pid == 0:
 516:         #  -T halts on a non-existent memory access rather than logging one
 517:         #  host line per access: a run that goes wrong stops instead of
 518:         #  flooding the pty and making the row host-speed dependent.
 519:         os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-A", "-T", "-E", "testarm", "-M", "64",
 520:                         "0x%x:%s" % (CODE, stub)])
 521:         os._exit(127)
 522:     buf = ""
 523: 
 524:     def rd(t=0.4):
 525:         nonlocal buf
 526:         r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], t)
 527:         if fd not in r:
 528:             return True
 529:         try:
 530:             d = os.read(fd, 65536)
 531:         except OSError:
 532:             return False
 533:         if not d:
 534:             return False
 535:         buf += d.decode("latin1", "replace")
 536:         return True
 537: 
 538:     def wait_from(mark, timeout=30):
 539:         t = time.time()
 540:         while time.time() - t < timeout:
 541:             if not rd():
 542:                 return False
 543:             if len(buf) > mark and buf[mark:].rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
 544:                 return True
 545:         return False
 546: 
 547:     def send(sx):
 548:         mark = len(buf)
 549:         b = (sx + "\n").encode("latin1")
 550:         n = 0
 551:         while n < len(b):
 552:             n += os.write(fd, b[n:])
 553:         return wait_from(mark)
 554: 
 555:     if not wait_from(0, 60):
 556:         os.kill(pid, 9)
 557:         os.waitpid(pid, 0)
 558:         return None
 559: 
 560:     #  Four distinct bytes per word, so a byte-order or rotation change would
 561:     #  be unmistakable rather than a plausible-looking value.
 562:     for i in range(8):
 563:         send("put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (SRC + 4 * i, 0x11223344 + i))
 564:     send("put w 0x0, 0x99887766")        # row K reads from address 0
 565: 
 566:     #  #364: the COLD page, laid down a BYTE AT A TIME on purpose. `put b` uses
 567:     #  CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS, and translation-array insertion is gated on
 568:     #  `!no_exceptions`, so this page is NOT warmed and a load from it takes the
 569:     #  general path -- which no other row in this file does. `put w` here would
 570:     #  silently convert these rows into three more fast-path rows.
 571:     #
 572:     #  Bytes ascending so the little-endian word reads 0x11223344, matching the
 573:     #  `put w` pages above: 0x44 at COLD+0 through 0x11 at COLD+3. Do NOT switch
 574:     #  the whole probe to `put b` -- that would move all 17 existing rows to the
 575:     #  general path and DELETE the fast-path coverage, which is currently the
 576:     #  only coverage those four sites have.
 577:     for i, b in enumerate((0x44, 0x33, 0x22, 0x11)):
 578:         send("put b 0x%x, 0x%02x" % (COLD + i, b))
 579: 
 580:     for i, iw in enumerate(prog):
 581:         send("put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (CODE + 4 * i, iw))
 582:     send("pc=0x%x" % CODE)
 583: 
 584:     #  The programs finish in microseconds and then spin at `b .`, so the two
 585:     #  second wait is ~six orders of magnitude of margin: the registers are
 586:     #  settled long before the interrupt, and no row's value depends on how far
 587:     #  execution got. The iteration count lives in r3, not in elapsed time.
 588:     cmark = len(buf)
 589:     os.write(fd, b"continue\n")
 590:     t = time.time()
 591:     while time.time() - t < 2.0:
 592:         rd(0.3)
 593:     if not (len(buf) > cmark and buf[cmark:].rstrip().endswith("GXemul>")):
 594:         os.write(fd, b"\x03")
 595:         wait_from(cmark, 15)
 596: 
 597:     out = {}
 598:     for rn in regs:
 599:         mark = len(buf)
 600:         send("print %s" % rn)
 601:         #  `print <reg>` answers with the bare value on its own line
 602:         #  ("0x11223344"), NOT "r0 = 0x...". An earlier revision grepped for
 603:         #  the "r0 = " form and scored every row DEAD -- a broken probe that
 604:         #  looks exactly like a broken emulator.
 605:         m = re.search(r"(?m)^0x([0-9a-fA-F]+)\s*$", buf[mark:])
 606:         if m:
 607:             out[rn] = int(m.group(1), 16)
 608: 
 609:     #  #367: how many times this row entered A__NAME__general, read from the
 610:     #  emulator's own counter via `tlbdump`. This is the round that stopped path
 611:     #  attribution being a claim in a comment: which path a row takes was wrong
 612:     #  three times running (#364's inverted seeding note, #364's draft
 613:     #  mis-attributing a writeback site to an offset form that compiles none, and
 614:     #  #365 finding the sites reached but undiscriminated), and each was
 615:     #  rediscovered with a throwaway marker and a scratch build.
 616:     #
 617:     #  PULL, not push: `tlbdump` prints only when asked, so it cannot disturb the
 618:     #  rows elsewhere in this battery that assert an ABSENCE of output, and it
 619:     #  cannot flood a free-running guest. It is read AFTER the registers so a
 620:     #  parse failure here cannot cost a register value.
 621:     #
 622:     #  A missing line is DEAD, never 0 -- 0 is the expected value for every warm
 623:     #  row, so defaulting to it would make a lost read indistinguishable from a
 624:     #  correct answer, which is the defect class this battery keeps finding.
 625:     mark = len(buf)
 626:     send("tlbdump")
 627:     m = re.search(r"arm\.ls_general\s*=\s*(\d+)", buf[mark:])
 628:     out["_lsgen"] = int(m.group(1)) if m else None
 629: 
 630:     try:
 631:         os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
 632:         time.sleep(0.2)
 633:         os.kill(pid, 9)
 634:         os.waitpid(pid, 0)
 635:     except Exception:
 636:         pass
 637:     return out
 638: 
 639: 
 640: #  Gate-convention output: one "<name>  ... ok" / "... FAIL" line per row, a
 641: #  RESULT= line, and a CONTROL= line proving the instrument is LIVE rather than
 642: #  merely quiet. The control is the loaded-data row: r1 starts at 0, so a
 643: #  distinctive NONZERO value proves the guest really executed and really loaded,
 644: #  which is the check this project adds after a committed row was found to be
 645: #  measuring the wrong register field -- a wrong field reads 0, and a row that
 646: #  accepts 0 accepts that mistake by accident.
 647: print("=== #19: ARM load/store base writeback (pre/post-index) ===")
 648: print("    buggy = masked base + offset ; arch = unmasked (A5.2.5 / A5.2.8)")
 649: print("    DISC-M pins the A5.3.6 pseudocode model, not a silicon mandate")
 650: 
 651: #  #367: the expected number of A__NAME__general entries per row. Derived from
 652: #  the template's control flow, NOT guessed, because a wrong expectation here
 653: #  becomes a new false red:
 654: #
 655: #      count = (page x needed-permission) upgrade events + T-form first touches
 656: #
 657: #  The general path's own memory_rw SELF-WARMS -- it inserts, setting host_load
 658: #  unconditionally, host_store whenever the access is granted write permission
 659: #  (#382 correction: these rigs run MMU-off, where update_translation_table's
 660: #  writeflag is ok-1 == 1 for ANY data access, so a LOAD warms host_store too;
 661: #  host_store is left NULL only MMU-on on a read-only page), and the is_userpage
 662: #  bit iff it was a user access. So:
 663: #    * every `put w`-seeded row is 0, including all ten iterations of the x10
 664: #      rows and both passes of the re-seeded row: the page is warm before the
 665: #      guest runs a single instruction;
 666: #    * the four `put b`-seeded cold rows are 1 -- one entry, which then warms;
 667: #    * the `ldrt` T-form row is 1 even though its page IS warm, because the
 668: #      is_userpage test precedes the page test and a kernel `put w` insert never
 669: #      sets that bit. Its own general call then SETS it, which is why a SECOND
 670: #      ldrt on that page would be fast.
 671: #  Non-obvious cases for whoever adds rows: a COLD x10 row is 1, not 0 and not
 672: #  10 -- iteration 1 warms via the general path's own insert. It is 10 only where
 673: #  insertion is BLOCKED (a device page, a partial page, MMU-on unmapped). A cold
 674: #  row doing a load then a store on one page is 1, not 2 (#382 correction): MMU
 675: #  off, the load's own insert already warms host_store (ok-1 == 1), so the store
 676: #  finds the page mapped -- it would be 2 only MMU-on on a read-only page.
