   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: """Gate 14's netbsd_memcpy instrument (#354), read-ahead install path.
   3: 
   4: Prints one line per row:  <name> <got> <want> ok|FAIL
   5: then MEMCPY_CONTROL=OK|DEAD and MEMCPY_RESULT=<passed>/<total>.
   6: gate_arm.sh does the asserting.
   7: 
   8: The defect (#354): X(netbsd_memcpy) folds the NetBSD/arm memcpy loop
   9: (ldmia r1!,{r3,r4,ip,lr} / stmia r0!,{...} x2 / subs r2,#0x20 / bge). It did
  10: memcpy(dst,src,32) per iteration and advanced r0/r1, but NEVER wrote
  11: r3/r4/ip/lr -- which the architecture leaves holding the LAST 16 bytes loaded
  12: (the final iteration's second ldmia). So after a memcpy the guest read four
  13: stale registers. #354 publishes them from page_1+(addr_r1&0xffc)+16, before the
  14: memcpy, direct read no byteswap (matching multi_0x08b15018).
