   1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
   2: """#432: the DC21285 "footbridge" driven by REAL GUEST INSTRUCTIONS, with no rig.
   3: 
   4: WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE regress/diff_footbridge.c.  The offline differential calls
   5: footbridge_effective_cycles() and dev_footbridge_access() directly, so it proves BEHAVIOUR
   6: but cannot prove the device is REACHABLE by a guest -- it would stay green under a change
   7: that unmapped it (a memory_device_register length, a DM_ flag, or machine_cats.c's
   8: `addr=0x42000000` line).  Until this probe, R6 was THE ONLY CODE ROUND IN THIS TREE WITH NO
   9: REACHABILITY WITNESS OF ANY KIND: no rig boots a footbridge and there is no m8online analog.
  10: This is that analog.  It goes through the real address decode and the real memory_rw
  11: plumbing, on a COLD DEBUGGER -- no boot, no disk image, no guest OS.
  12: 
  13: *** THE TRAP THIS PROBE EXISTS TO SURVIVE, and it is the whole reason for arm T. ***
  14: machine_cats.c:131 calls arm_setup_initial_translation_table(), which (cpu_arm.c:239-255)
  15: writes a section descriptor for i in 0..255 at VA (j<<28)+(i<<20) -> PA i<<20 -- i.e. the
  16: TOP NIBBLE OF THE VA IS IGNORED and only the low 256 MB of physical space is mapped
  17: anywhere.  The footbridge sits at PA 0x42000000, outside that window, so under the boot-time
  18: MMU it is UNREACHABLE: VA 0x42000000 aliases to PA 0x02000000 and a load returns 0.
  19: 
  20: That is not a hypothetical.  The first version of this probe returned 0x0 on every device
  21: row WITH ITS RAM CONTROL STILL GREEN, and 0x0 is exactly what "the device is not there"
  22: looks like -- so a survival-shaped or absence-shaped assertion would have CONFIRMED THE
  23: OPPOSITE CONCLUSION.  A RAM liveness row cannot see it, because VA 0x9000 maps to PA 0x9000
  24: identically with the MMU on or off.
  25: 
  26: So the guard is not a comment, it is arm T: THE SAME PROGRAM WITH THE THREE MMU-DISABLE
  27: INSTRUCTIONS REPLACED BY NOPS MUST NOT PRODUCE THE DEVICE'S ANSWER.  If arm T ever reads
  28: 0x1011, the discriminator is dead and every other row here is meaningless -- and it goes RED
  29: rather than silently green.  Arm L additionally reads CP15 c1 BACK after the mcr and asserts
  30: bit 0 is clear, so the precondition is MEASURED rather than inferred from the store having
  31: been issued.  Three separate statements, deliberately: the guest turned the MMU off, the two
  32: arms really differ in MMU state, and only the MMU-off arm can see the device.
  33: 
  34: VALUES, NOT SURVIVAL (rounds 79/80, and m8820x_sites_probe.py's header says the same).
  35: A row that only asks "did the host stay up" cannot tell a reachable device from an absent
  36: one -- an unmapped read survives too, it just returns 0.  So the two device rows assert
  37: values that ONLY dev_footbridge.c can produce:
  38:   * A1 reads VENDOR_ID and requires 0x1011 (dev_footbridge.c:423, DC21285_VENDOR_ID).
  39:     Nonzero and specific: the hand-assembled-encoding trap this project has been bitten by
  40:     yields 0 from a wrong register field, and 0 is the one answer this row rejects.
  41:   * A2 writes 0x12345678 to TIMER_1_LOAD and reads back 0x00345678.  THE VANISHING TOP BYTE
  42:     IS THE HANDLER'S OWN SIGNATURE: dev_footbridge.c:548 stores `idata & TIMER_MAX_VAL`
  43:     (0x00FFFFFF, dc21285reg.h:371).  RAM, or any decode that merely absorbed the store,
  44:     would return 0x12345678 unchanged.  This row therefore distinguishes "the device
  45:     handled it" from "the write landed somewhere", which A1 alone cannot.
  46: 
  47: THE KILL PAIR, which is this probe's own failability control.  Arms L and K differ by
  48: EXACTLY ONE INSTRUCTION.  Arm K adds a guest read of IRQ_ENABLE_SET (base+0x188), which
  49: dev_footbridge.c:495-497 answers with fatal() then exit(1) -- an UNFIXED site, used here
  50: rather than repaired, because a probe that reports SURVIVED for every row is vacuous unless
  51: something in it can still die.  Arm L must live; arm K must exit 1 and say so.  If both
  52: survived, the guest instructions are not reaching the device at all and every SURVIVED
  53: verdict above is worthless; if both died, the probe is killing the host for its own reasons.
  54: The one-instruction delta is the entire argument.
  55: 
  56: EVERY PLANTED WORD IS CHECKED AGAINST THE DISASSEMBLY THE EMULATOR PRINTS AS IT STEPS IT --
  57: not against a separate `unassemble`, because the step line is the instruction that actually
  58: executed.  This project has a recorded incident where a wrong register field made a gate row
  59: measure the wrong thing for months.
  60: 
  61: COST, measured: see PROBE_WALL below -- 3 cold-debugger launches, no boot.  A booting-rig row
  62: was deliberately NOT written: no rig in this tree boots a footbridge machine at all, which is
  63: the gap this probe closes, and adding one would put a load-sensitive wall-clock oracle into a
  64: battery already carrying ~37.
  65: 
  66: usage: footbridge_sites_probe.py <gxemul-binary> <four-byte-raw-stub>
  67: The stub is the same scaffolding gate_arm.sh writes for testarm: `cats` needs a file
  68: argument to construct, and every word of it is overwritten before anything executes.
  69: """
  70: import os, pty, re, select, sys, time
  71: 
  72: BIN = sys.argv[1]
  73: STUB = sys.argv[2]
  74: CODE = 0x00008000
  75: DATA = 0x00009000
  76: NOP = 0xE1A00000
  77: NOPTXT = "mov r0,r0"      #  the EMULATOR's spelling of 0xe1a00000; it does not print "nop"
  78: 
  79: #  (offset, word, expected disassembly text, comment)
  80: #  Spellings are the EMULATOR's own, not a manual's: "mrc 15,0,r0,cr1,cr0,0" and decimal
  81: #  ldr/str offsets ("[r0,#768]" for 0x300) are what this disassembler prints.
  82: MMU_OFF = [
  83:     (0x00, 0xEE110F10, "mrc 15,0,r0,cr1,cr0,0", "read CP15 c1"),
  84:     (0x04, 0xE3C00001, "bic r0,r0,#1",          "clear the MMU enable bit"),
  85:     (0x08, 0xEE010F10, "mcr 15,0,r0,cr1,cr0,0", "MMU OFF -> VA == PA"),
  86: ]
  87: MMU_NOP = [
  88:     (0x00, NOP, NOPTXT, "arm T: MMU LEFT ON -- the negative control"),
  89:     (0x04, NOP, NOPTXT, ""),
  90:     (0x08, NOP, NOPTXT, ""),
  91: ]
  92: BODY = [
  93:     (0x0c, 0xEE113F10, "mrc 15,0,r3,cr1,cr0,0", "C2: read CP15 c1 BACK -- measures the state"),
  94:     (0x10, 0xE3A00442, "mov r0,#0x42000000",    "footbridge base (machine_cats.c:64)"),
  95:     (0x14, 0xE3A04A09, "mov r4,#0x9000",        "scratch RAM"),
  96:     (0x18, 0xE5945000, "ldr r5,[r4]",           "C1 liveness: plain RAM, 0x11223344"),
  97:     (0x1c, 0xE5906000, "ldr r6,[r0]",           "A1: VENDOR_ID"),
  98:     (0x20, 0xE5942004, "ldr r2,[r4,#4]",        "load 0x12345678 from RAM"),
  99:     (0x24, 0xE5802300, "str r2,[r0,#768]",      "A2: TIMER_1_LOAD <- 0x12345678"),
 100:     (0x28, 0xE5901300, "ldr r1,[r0,#768]",      "A2: read it back"),
 101: ]
 102: KILL_NOP  = (0x2c, NOP,        NOPTXT,             "arm L: no kill site")
 103: KILL_LOAD = (0x2c, 0xE5907188, "ldr r7,[r0,#392]", "arm K: read IRQ_ENABLE_SET (0x188)")
 104: TAIL = [(0x30, NOP, NOPTXT, "landing pad -- planted, never stepped")]
 105: 
 106: 
 107: def run_arm(label, prog):
 108:     """Plant `prog`, step it one instruction at a time, report registers and liveness."""
 109:     pid, fd = pty.fork()
 110:     if pid == 0:
 111:         os.execvp(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-T", "-E", "cats", "-M", "32",
 112:                         "0x%x:%s" % (CODE, STUB)])
 113:         os._exit(127)
 114:     buf = ""
 115:     dead = [False]
 116: 
 117:     def rd(t=0.4):
 118:         nonlocal buf
 119:         r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], t)
 120:         if fd not in r:
 121:             return True
 122:         try:
 123:             d = os.read(fd, 65536)
 124:         except OSError:
 125:             dead[0] = True
 126:             return False
 127:         if not d:
 128:             dead[0] = True
 129:             return False
 130:         buf += d.decode("latin1", "replace")
 131:         return True
 132: 
 133:     #  Signature order is not free choice: gate_hygiene.sh pins the CANONICAL #392 call form
 134:     #  as a literal `return wait(mark=_mark, echo=<x> if <x> else None)`, tightened three
 135:     #  times because every looser prefix was a substring of the broken form too.  Written any
 136:     #  other way, a probe is simply not covered by that ratchet -- which is worse than failing
 137:     #  it.  So timeout goes last, and send() ends on the pinned form.
 138:     def wait(mark=0, echo=None, timeout=60):
 139:         t = time.time()
 140:         while time.time() - t < timeout:
 141:             if not rd():
 142:                 return False
 143:             resp = buf[mark:]
 144:             if echo is not None and echo not in resp:
 145:                 continue
 146:             if len(buf) > mark and resp.rstrip().endswith("GXemul>"):
 147:                 return True
 148:         return False
 149: 
 150:     def send(x, timeout=60):
 151:         b = (x + "\n").encode("latin1")
 152:         _mark = len(buf)
 153:         n = 0
 154:         try:
 155:             while n < len(b):
 156:                 n += os.write(fd, b[n:])
 157:         except OSError:
 158:             dead[0] = True
 159:             return False
 160:         return wait(mark=_mark, echo=x if x else None)
 161: 
 162:     status, regs, badis = "?", {}, []
 163:     #  KEYWORD, and this is not style.  A positional wait(120) silently becomes mark=120 under
 164:     #  the pinned parameter order -- a real behaviour change, and the exact mistake the
 165:     #  m8820x probe's conversion had to fix in the same commit.
 166:     if not wait(timeout=120):
 167:         status = "NO-PROMPT"
 168:     else:
 169:         send("put w 0x%08x, 0x%08x" % (DATA, 0x11223344))
 170:         send("put w 0x%08x, 0x%08x" % (DATA + 4, 0x12345678))
 171:         for off, word, _t, _c in prog:
 172:             send("put w 0x%08x, 0x%08x" % (CODE + off, word))
 173:         send("pc=0x%08x" % CODE)
 174:         status = "SURVIVED"
 175:         for off, word, text, _c in prog[:-1]:      # the landing pad is never stepped
 176:             mark = len(buf)
 177:             if not send("step 1", timeout=30) or dead[0]:
 178:                 status = "HOST-DIED"
 179:                 break
 180:             seg = buf[mark:]
 181:             #  Match the emulator's own step line for THIS address and compare the mnemonic
 182:             #  it printed against what this table says was planted.
 183:             m = re.search(r"%08x:\s+%08x\s+([^\r\n<]+)" % (CODE + off, word), seg)
 184:             got = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", m.group(1)).strip() if m else "(no step line)"
 185:             if got != text:
 186:                 badis.append("0x%02x planted 0x%08x -> %r, expected %r"
 187:                              % (off, word, got, text))
 188:         if status == "SURVIVED":
 189:             mark = len(buf)
 190:             if send("reg", timeout=30) and not dead[0]:
 191:                 for n in (0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7):
 192:                     mm = re.search(r"\br%d\s*=\s*0x([0-9a-fA-F]+)" % n, buf[mark:])
 193:                     if mm:
 194:                         regs[n] = int(mm.group(1), 16)
 195:             else:
 196:                 status = "HOST-DIED" if dead[0] else "NO-REG"
 197:     fatals = re.findall(r"(\[[^\r\n\]]*footbridge[^\r\n\]]*\])", buf)
 198:     try:
 199:         os.write(fd, b"quit\n")
 200:         time.sleep(0.3)
 201:     except OSError:
 202:         pass
 203:     try:
 204:         os.close(fd)
 205:     except OSError:
 206:         pass
 207:     try:
 208:         _, st = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
 209:     except Exception:
 210:         st = None
 211:     code = os.WEXITSTATUS(st) if st is not None and os.WIFEXITED(st) else None
 212:     sig = os.WTERMSIG(st) if st is not None and os.WIFSIGNALED(st) else None
 213:     print("  arm %-36s %-9s exit=%-5s signal=%s" % (label, status, code, sig))
 214:     for b in badis:
 215:         print("      DISASSEMBLY MISMATCH: %s" % b)
 216:     return {"status": status, "regs": regs, "exit": code, "signal": sig,
 217:             "fatals": fatals, "badis": badis}
 218: 
 219: 
 220: def hexs(v):
 221:     return "-" if v is None else "0x%08x" % v
 222: 
 223: 
 224: def row(tag, note, got, want):
 225:     ok = got == want
 226:     print("%-4s %-40s got=%-12s want=%-12s %s"
 227:           % (tag, note, hexs(got), hexs(want), "ok" if ok else "FAIL"))
 228:     return ok
 229: 
 230: 
 231: print("BINARY=%s" % BIN)
 232: print("machine: cats/SA110, cold debugger, MMU disabled by the guest, no boot")
 233: t0 = time.time()
 234: 
 235: L = run_arm("L  live      (MMU off)",      MMU_OFF + BODY + [KILL_NOP] + TAIL)
 236: T = run_arm("T  trap ctrl (MMU LEFT ON)",  MMU_NOP + BODY + [KILL_NOP] + TAIL)
 237: K = run_arm("K  kill      (MMU off,+188)", MMU_OFF + BODY + [KILL_LOAD] + TAIL)
 238: print()
 239: 
 240: results = []
 241: #  ---- controls.  A probe whose control row failed has measured NOTHING. ----
 242: c1 = row("C1", "liveness: guest ran, plain RAM readback",
 243:          L["regs"].get(5), 0x11223344)
 244: c2 = row("C2", "MMU really OFF in arm L (CP15 c1 bit 0)",
 245:          (L["regs"][3] & 1) if 3 in L["regs"] else None, 0)
 246: c3 = row("C3", "MMU really ON in arm T (the arms differ)",
 247:          (T["regs"][3] & 1) if 3 in T["regs"] else None, 1)
 248: results += [c1, c2, c3]
 249: #  *** C4 IS THE TRAP GUARD.  Stated as an INEQUALITY on purpose: arm T may read 0, or -T may
 250: #  halt it on the non-existent alias at PA 0x02000000, and both are acceptable outcomes.  What
 251: #  is never acceptable is arm T producing the device's answer, because then arm L's 0x1011 is
 252: #  not evidence of anything.  ***
 253: t_vendor = T["regs"].get(6)
 254: c4 = t_vendor != 0x1011
 255: print("%-4s %-40s got=%-12s want=%-12s %s"
 256:       % ("C4", "MMU-on arm CANNOT see the device", hexs(t_vendor), "!= 0x1011",
 257:          "ok" if c4 else "FAIL"))
 258: if not c4:
 259:     print("      *** THE DISCRIMINATOR IS DEAD: the device answered with the boot-time MMU")
 260:     print("      *** still enabled, so arm L proves nothing about turning it off.  Either the")
 261:     print("      *** cats translation table changed (machine_cats.c:131, cpu_arm.c:239-255)")
 262:     print("      *** or the device moved into the low 256 MB.  Re-derive before believing A1/A2.")
 263: results.append(c4)
 264: 
 265: #  ---- the reachability rows.  VALUES, not survival. ----
 266: a1 = row("A1", "VENDOR_ID through a real guest ldr", L["regs"].get(6), 0x1011)
 267: a2 = row("A2", "TIMER_1_LOAD masked to 24 bits by :548", L["regs"].get(1), 0x00345678)
 268: results.append(a1)
 269: results.append(a2)
 270: #  *** NAME THE RIGHT CAUSE, and this branch is here because the first draft named the WRONG
 271: #  one.  A red A1 has two utterly different causes that look identical -- r6 == 0 -- and the
 272: #  earlier version printed "this is NOT the MMU trap" unconditionally, so on the very run
 273: #  where the MMU trap WAS the cause it pointed the reader at the device.  Measured: the
 274: #  negative-control run (arm L with the three mcr/bic words replaced by nops) printed exactly
 275: #  that misdiagnosis.  A diagnostic that fires on the wrong branch is worse than none.  ***
 276: if not a1:
 277:     if not c2:
 278:         print("      *** THE MMU TRAP, and A1/A2 are NOT evidence about the device: arm L's")
 279:         print("      *** CP15 c1 bit 0 is still SET, so VA 0x42000000 aliased to PA 0x02000000")
 280:         print("      *** (machine_cats.c:131 -> cpu_arm.c:239-255 maps only the low 256 MB) and")
 281:         print("      *** nothing ever addressed the footbridge.  Fix the MMU-off sequence first.")
 282:     elif L["regs"].get(6) == 0:
 283:         print("      NOTE: r6 == 0 with C2 and C4 GREEN is not the MMU trap -- the guest really")
 284:         print("      NOTE: did address PA 0x42000000, so the device is absent or has stopped")
 285:         print("      NOTE: decoding there (memory_device_register / DM_ flags / machine_cats.c:64).")
 286: results.append(row("A2b", "the value the guest actually stored",
 287:                    L["regs"].get(2), 0x12345678))
 288: 
 289: #  ---- the kill pair: this probe's own failability control. ----
 290: l_ok = L["status"] == "SURVIVED" and L["exit"] == 0 and not L["fatals"]
 291: k_ok = any("ENABLE SET" in f for f in K["fatals"]) and K["exit"] == 1
 292: print("%-4s %-40s %-30s %s"
 293:       % ("I1", "isolation arm SURVIVES untouched",
 294:          "status=%s exit=%s" % (L["status"], L["exit"]), "ok" if l_ok else "FAIL"))
 295: print("%-4s %-40s %-30s %s"
 296:       % ("K1", "kill arm dies at 0x188, exit 1",
 297:          "exit=%s" % K["exit"], "ok" if k_ok else "FAIL"))
 298: print("      kill arm said: %s" % ((K["fatals"] or ["(nothing)"])[-1]))
 299: results.append(l_ok)
 300: results.append(k_ok)
 301: 
 302: nbad = sum(len(a["badis"]) for a in (L, T, K))
 303: print("%-4s %-40s %-30s %s"
 304:       % ("D1", "every planted word disassembled as meant",
 305:          "%d mismatches" % nbad, "ok" if nbad == 0 else "FAIL"))
 306: results.append(nbad == 0)
 307: 
 308: #  Named, not indexed.  The first version wrote `results[0] and results[1] ...`, which silently
 309: #  re-points at different rows the moment a row is inserted above -- the same class as pinning a
 310: #  row by an id that names a family.
 311: ctrl_ok = c1 and c2 and c3 and c4
 312: print()
 313: print("ROWS=%d of %d" % (sum(1 for r in results if r), len(results)))
 314: print("FOOTBRIDGE_SITES_CONTROL=%s" % ("OK" if ctrl_ok else "BAD"))
 315: print("FOOTBRIDGE_SITES_%s" % ("PASS" if all(results) else "FAIL"))
 316: print("PROBE_WALL=%.1fs for 3 cold-debugger arms" % (time.time() - t0))

